{"id": "live-150", "title": "State of Idaho, Idaho Transportation Board v. Triple Crown Development, LLC (Business-Damages Condemnation Appeal)", "category": "eminent-domain", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-09-14", "jurisdiction": "Idaho Supreme Court", "state": "ID", "amount": "Business-damages claim rejected; underlying land value stipulated separately", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.mealeys.com/mealeys/mealeys-attorney-fees/articles/2524720", "summary": "The Idaho Supreme Court affirmed summary judgment against business-damages claims brought by Triple Crown Development, LLC and affiliated Thueson-controlled entities after the state condemned Triple Crown's land in Canyon County for a highway interchange. The Court held that Idaho Code section 7-711(2)'s business-damages remedy belongs only to the fee owner of the condemned parcel, not commonly controlled businesses on adjoining land, and separately rejected the state's request for attorney fees.", "significance": "Clarifies that Idaho's statutory business-damages remedy tracks legal title to the condemned parcel rather than economic interdependence among commonly controlled entities, and confirms that a never-permitted, never-operated business plan cannot support a business-damages award even for the actual fee owner.", "judge": null, "tags": ["eminent domain", "business damages", "idaho", "condemnation", "highway interchange"]}
